# inspectord-managed rkhunter drop-in — managed file, do not edit.
#
# WHERE THIS GOES: /etc/rkhunter.d/inspectord.conf
#
# rkhunter 1.4.6 reads every `*.conf` in a directory named `rkhunter.d` that
# sits BESIDE its main config file — so /etc/rkhunter.d/ for /etc/rkhunter.conf.
# It is NOT `/etc/rkhunter.conf.d/`, which parent spec §30.6 names: rkhunter
# never looks there. Verified two ways against the installed 1.4.6:
#   * /usr/bin/rkhunter sets `LOCALCONFIGDIR="${configdir}/rkhunter.d"` and then
#     globs `${LOCALCONFIGDIR}/*.conf` (and skips symlinks and unreadable files);
#   * a live run logs `Info: Using local configuration directory
#     '<dir>/rkhunter.d': 1 file found`.
# The header of /etc/rkhunter.conf says the same in prose.
#
# WHY: on Arch and CachyOS these three files genuinely ARE shell scripts.
# /usr/bin/egrep and /usr/bin/fgrep are POSIX shell wrappers around `grep -E` /
# `grep -F`, and /usr/bin/ldd is a bash script. rkhunter's file-properties test
# reports each as "has been replaced by a script", which is true and, here,
# entirely expected — three `medium` alerts every single night that can never be
# resolved, because the distro is not going to stop shipping wrappers.
#
# ============================ READ THIS ============================
# WHAT SCRIPTWHITELIST SUPPRESSES — and, more importantly, what it does NOT.
#
# It suppresses ONE check, the "script replacement" check, for ONLY the exact
# paths listed below. It is not a general exemption and it does not remove these
# files from monitoring.
#
# Evidence, from /usr/bin/rkhunter (1.4.6) itself. SCRIPTWHITELIST is consulted
# at exactly one place in the whole program, inside the `if [ $SKIP_SCRIPT -eq
# 0 ]` block that implements the script-replacement check:
#
#     if [ -n "`echo \"${SCRIPTWHITELIST}\" | grep \"^${FNAMEGREP}$\"`" ]; then
#             WL_FILE="whitelisted"
#             WHITELIST_RESULT="${WHITELIST_RESULT} script"
#     fi
#     if [ -z "${WL_FILE}" ]; then
#             ... run the `file` command and, if it says "script",
#                 TEST_RESULT="${TEST_RESULT} script"
#     fi
#
# A match therefore does two things and nothing else: it skips the `file`
# invocation, and it appends " script" to WHITELIST_RESULT — a variable that is
# only ever written to the log at verbose level, never to a warning. The hash,
# permission, uid, gid, inode, size, mtime and symlink-target comparisons are
# separate blocks that append to TEST_RESULT independently, and rkhunter warns
# whenever TEST_RESULT is non-empty. Each of those has its OWN whitelist
# (ATTRWHITELIST, WRITEWHITELIST, IMMUTWHITELIST) that this file does not touch.
#
# A live run confirms the scoping in the log, per file:
#     Info: Found file '/usr/bin/egrep': it is whitelisted for the
#           'script replacement' check.
#
# So: if someone replaces /usr/bin/ldd with a malicious script, rkhunter will
# still report it — as a CHANGED HASH, not as a script replacement. That
# fallback only exists once a baseline exists. `/var/lib/rkhunter/db/rkhunter.dat`
# is NOT shipped by the Arch package, and inspectord will never create it
# (`rkhunter --propupd` certifies the machine as it is right now — that is the
# user's call, not a daemon's). Until you run `rkhunter --propupd` on a machine
# you believe is clean, the properties comparison does not run at all and these
# three files are effectively unmonitored by rkhunter.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT SUPPRESS: the two remaining warnings on a stock host —
# the missing rkhunter.dat baseline and rkhunter's standing --propupd
# responsibility notice. Measured on a healthy Arch/CachyOS box with
# `--check --enable properties --report-warnings-only`: 5 warnings without this
# file, 2 with it. Only `rkhunter --propupd` retires the other two.
# ==================================================================
#
# `rkhunter --config-check` exits 0 with this file in place (measured).
SCRIPTWHITELIST=/usr/bin/egrep
SCRIPTWHITELIST=/usr/bin/fgrep
SCRIPTWHITELIST=/usr/bin/ldd
